function.body:
v_cost NUMBER; BEGIN -- ETP-5446. Current cost of the product, as the costing engine records it in -- M_Costing. Feeds EM_ETGO_Cost (Computation_Mode = 'S', Refresh_Mode = 'S'). -- -- TOTAL FUNCTION. Refresh_Mode = 'S' runs this inside the business transaction -- that wrote M_Costing (a cost line saved from the Product window, or a costing -- background run), so ANY exception raised here would roll that transaction back. -- Every edge case below resolves to a value, never to an error. -- -- Definition - follows the cost types and datefrom/dateto ordering of core's -- "latest cost" selection (M_GET_NO_TRX_PRD_COST_LATEST, core Issue #641), but -- ranks started rows (datefrom <= now()) first and differs on the warehouse -- tie-break (see edge case 3 below): -- * cost types STA (standard) and AVA (average) only - the two types the -- current costing engine writes; legacy ST/AV rows predate it; -- * non-null cost, datefrom and dateto (core's own predicates reject nulls); -- * rows that have already started (datefrom <= now()) before future-dated -- ones, then the most recent datefrom, then the longest-lived dateto. -- Same rule as the purchase/sale price columns (ETGO_PRODUCT_PURCHASE_PRICE -- orders by validfrom <= now() first): the cost in force today wins over a cost -- scheduled for a later date. A future-dated row is only picked when no row of -- the product has started yet (a scheduled cost beats an empty cell). A row -- closed with an expiry date (dateto) and no successor is still shown. -- -- Known, accepted staleness - the only clock read is that now(), and a stored -- value is recomputed only when an M_Costing row of the product changes. If a -- future-dated cost reaches its start date and nothing touches that product's -- costing, the column keeps the previous cost until the next M_Costing change -- (or an ad_scd_rebuild). The same behavior the purchase/sale price columns -- already accept. The engine's own rows always start at the transaction date, -- so this only affects manual costs dated in the future. -- -- The value is per product, not per organization or warehouse, and it is the raw -- M_Costing.Cost in the row's own currency (no conversion - a conversion depends on -- a rate date and would add a second, unwatched clock dependency).
-- Edge case 1 - null target id. Cannot happen through the engine (it always -- passes a PK); answer "no cost" rather than let the NULL travel. IF p_m_product_id IS NULL THEN RETURN NULL; END IF;
-- Edge case 2 - no cost row at all (TC3): SELECT ... INTO assigns NULL for zero -- rows and does not raise (no INTO STRICT on purpose). The list shows it empty. -- Edge case 3 - several rows. LIMIT 1 keeps it single-valued, and the tail of the -- ORDER BY makes the pick DETERMINISTIC: the engine writes rows that share the -- same datefrom/dateto, and it writes the value unconditionally on every -- recompute, so an arbitrary pick would make ad_scd_check report phantom drift. -- An organization-wide row (no warehouse) is preferred over a warehouse-specific -- one because this value has no warehouse context. This is the OPPOSITE of core, -- which puts warehouse rows first on a datefrom/dateto tie - intentional. -- Filters through M_COSTING_PRODUCT_DATE (m_product_id, ...); the leading -- now() key means that product's rows are sorted instead of read in index -- order, which is bounded by the per-product row count. SELECT c.cost INTO v_cost FROM m_costing c WHERE c.m_product_id = p_m_product_id AND c.costtype IN ('STA', 'AVA') AND c.cost IS NOT NULL AND c.datefrom IS NOT NULL AND c.dateto IS NOT NULL ORDER BY (c.datefrom <= now()) DESC, c.datefrom DESC, c.dateto DESC, CASE WHEN c.m_warehouse_id IS NULL THEN 0 ELSE 1 END, c.created DESC, c.m_costing_id DESC LIMIT 1;
RETURN v_cost;
-- Belt and braces. Nothing above should be able to raise, but an unforeseen runtime -- error must not take a cost save or a costing run down with it. "No cost" is -- visible and recoverable (ad_scd_rebuild); a failed save is not. EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN RETURN NULL; END ETGO_PRODUCT_COST
